# Design

- Regarding the initial design, I just generated a quick to use logo with a theme palette I found online and created a palette image (assets\palette\colour-palette.png) to follow.
- I made the decision to seperate styling segment, such colours,typography, into their own individual parts, keeping global as the pure structure. I like this for multiple reasons, including semantic naming and potential avenues for shared brand configuration (Should this be a multi-brand product down the line)
- Planning the layout now, I have looked at a few existing designs now (documentation\Design Research) just to get an idea for the feel.
- I have decided to use CSS Modules, for better file following and styling practices. This is a personal preference.

# Architecture

- Folder/File Structure, I decided to go for a simplified structure, easy to read, easy to understand at all levels of development - I am very much mindset at ensuring a codebase is entry-level friendly, without over complicated terms and structures that would confuse anyone.
- State Management - I decided on Jotai, this is the global react state management I am most familiar with on a commercial level.
- Shell structure - I always have developer the app layout starting with a shell, this helps me get a skeleton layout I can start developing over, Normally, I would seperate the shell into its own layout, however due to this working as a single page application, the App.tsx should be sufficient to hold the shell structure.

# Notes

- Exploring the weatherstackAPI Documentation, I found the weather stack playground to be extremely useful (https://docs.apilayer.com/weatherstack/docs/weatherstack-api-v-1-0-0)
- For the Purpose of this project, I have prefixed variables with VITE*... unprefixed variables are never made available to the browser bundle, as a security default and since this project has no backend to keep the API key server-side, the variables are pretty much exposed and the VITE* prefix doesn't cause that exposure.
- One thing I wasnt sure on, until I teste dthe getCurrentWeather was that the response included a proper date response, I was worried that when I would query the locations local time, if someone in South Africa searches for "Tokyo," the date should reflect Tokyo's local day, not their own. This was confirmed to be returned correctly through location.localtime_epoch and localtime, and since localtime is provided as a plain string, I will use this.

